{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mw00/project-maya/pulls/624","id":5054725358,"node_id":"PR_kwDOU-xXos8AAAABLUj87g","html_url":"https://github.com/mw00/project-maya/pull/624","diff_url":"https://github.com/mw00/project-maya/pull/624.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/mw00/project-maya/pull/624.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mw00/project-maya/issues/624","commits_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mw00/project-maya/pulls/624/commits","review_comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mw00/project-maya/pulls/624/comments","review_comment_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mw00/project-maya/pulls/comments{/number}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mw00/project-maya/issues/624/comments","statuses_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mw00/project-maya/statuses/1872501868fee0a1c40b13de76fa7b4380ab2abf","number":624,"state":"open","locked":false,"title":"ds4 GGUF: Q5_K GEMVs of >= N activations on int8 tensor cores (DS4_Q5K_IMMA, opt-in, sm_80+), bit for bit the dp4a kernel","user":{"login":"SeriousRising","id":69419008,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5NDE5MDA4","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/69419008?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/SeriousRising","html_url":"https://github.com/SeriousRising","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SeriousRising/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SeriousRising/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SeriousRising/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SeriousRising/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SeriousRising/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SeriousRising/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SeriousRising/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SeriousRising/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/SeriousRising/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**What.** On GGUF packs like DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash GSQ-RCO, the dense matrices that run once per verify-window row are **Q5_K**: wq_b, wo_a, wo_b, the shared expert, Engram wkv and the head. They go through `gg::mv` -> `iq::mv_nc` -> `mv_kernel<kQ5_K, NC>` (dp4a). That kernel is not arithmetic-bound: at one column it already runs at 2.5-3.5x the weight-read floor, and each extra column costs ~27 µs on wq_b and ~390 µs on the head, because every warp re-reads the activations and redoes the half sums and the F32 epilogue per row. A verify window of T rows pays that T times.\n\n`mv_q5k_imma_kernel<NG>` (new, after `mv_kernel` in `src/ds4/ds4_iq.cu`): a block of 4 warps over 16 rows x 8 activations (NG = 2 for 9-16):\n\n- one `mma.m16n8k32.s8` a 32-value sub-block gives the exact int32 dots;\n- a second one against a ones matrix gives the activations' two half sums (the Q5_K min term);\n- each thread loads a whole super-block's bytes before the math.\n\n`mv_nc` dispatches to it for Q5_K when every job has >= N activations, 16-byte-aligned rows and 8-byte-aligned activations, on sm_80+ and not HIP (a `STRATA_USE_HIP` build keeps the dp4a kernel; NVIDIA's path is unchanged with the setting off).\n\n**Setting:** `MAYA_DS4_Q5K_IMMA=N` (the engine reads `STRATA_DS4_Q5K_IMMA`, then `DS4_Q5K_IMMA`), **off by default**: unset or 0 = today's kernel. A start line says when it is on. A section and row in `docs/SETTINGS.md`. At one activation the tensor-core kernel is slower, so it is a threshold, not a replacement; `2` is the value measured below.\n\n**Output: unchanged (bit-identical).** The integer dots are exact in any order (k is permuted the same way in A and B). Each output's F32 work is `mv_kernel`'s, operation for operation: `sub_dot<kQ5_K>`'s `__fmul_rn(d, (float)(sc * dot)) - (m * sl + m * sh)` per (row, column, sub-block); `fmaf(xd, v, acc)` into lane kb % 32's running sum in kb order; `warp_sum`'s xor tree over the 32 sums; `alpha * v + bias`. Warp q keeps the running sums of the super-blocks with sb % 4 == q in registers, and the tree reads all 32 from shared memory. `ds4_gguf_test --imma` (also in the full suite) compares it with the dp4a kernel at every activation count 1-16 on three shapes, memcmp-equal: Q5_K 136 x 768 (rows past a tile, < 32 sub-blocks), 61 x 1280 (several lane rounds, a partial tile), 40 x 5120 with bias and alpha 0.5.\n\n**Measured.** 2x NVIDIA CMP 170HX 64 GB (GA100, sm_80, CUDA 12.8; card 0 PCIe Gen2 x16, card 1 Gen2 x4: a mining card, platform-specific), 16-vCPU Xeon E5-2697 v4 VM. DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017 GSQ-RCO IQ2_XXS, layer split CUDA0 [0,20) / CUDA1 [20,40) + head, `--tp 1 --gpus 0,1 --dspark on --cpu-threads 14 --suffix-draft 0`.\n\nThe `maya-ds4` CLI on this branch, 2000 fixed prompt ids + 256 greedy tokens, one binary, one run each (a quick check):\n\n| | unset | `DS4_Q5K_IMMA=2` |\n|---|---|---|\n| token ids, DSpark counts | = v1.1.35 | = v1.1.35 (bit-identical) |\n| verifying, ms over the 80 rounds | 5024.1 | **4429.1 (-11.8 %)** |\n| drafting, ms a round | 6.25 | 5.08 (the drafter's head rows are several-column Q5_K GEMVs too) |\n| decode tok/s | 41.11 | 46.16 (+12 %) |\n\nPer call, `ds4_gguf_test --bench-cols`, CUDA0, µs, dp4a / tensor cores. On this branch: wq_b 32768 x 1280 at 1 / 2 / 4 / 8 columns 77.3 / 90.6, 113.8 / 120.9, 183.7 / 122.3, 325.9 / 114.5; wo_b 5120 x 8192 at 2 / 8: 79.7 / 75.5, 265.2 / 77.0; head 129280 x 5120 at 2 / 8: 1158.6 / 760.4, 3511.9 / 783.7. So at 2 columns wq_b is still slower on the tensor cores and the other shapes are faster. The full sweep, on our v1.1.30-based fork (back to back on one GPU; absolute values drift between runs, the ratios hold):\n\n| Q5_K shape (n x k) | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 6 | 8 | 16 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| wq_b 32768 x 1280 | 60.2 / 72.8 | 89.0 / 94.8 | 116.0 / 95.5 | 143.2 / 96.1 | 203.0 / 99.5 | 258.6 / 100.6 | 486.0 / 191.5 |\n| wo_b 5120 x 8192 | 53.7 / 71.4 | 82.0 / 77.1 | 109.6 / 77.6 | 136.9 / 78.3 | 205.6 / 78.3 | 270.2 / 80.1 | 513.0 / 114.2 |\n| 1024 x 5120 | 12.6 / 15.7 | 16.8 / 17.4 | 20.7 / 18.2 | 24.5 / 18.3 | 33.7 / 18.7 | 41.8 / 19.0 | 74.0 / 24.8 |\n| head 129280 x 5120 | 878 / 702 | 1168 / 850 | | 1901 / 884 | 2640 / 919 | 3436 / 953 | 6591 / 1568 |\n\nThrough the server on that fork (5 prompts x 512 tokens, temperature 0, thinking off; ms a verify window from the engine's timer; its other patches the same in every arm): off 41.96 ms, 46.32 tok/s; `=3` 39.24 ms (-6.5 %); `=2` **38.77 ms (-7.6 %)**, 50.14 tok/s; the same text hashes. `--dspark none` (every step one column): 24.46 vs 24.40 ms a step, unchanged.\n\n**Hardware classes.** sm_80 (2x CMP 170HX): measured above. Other sm_80+ (A100, RTX 30 / 40, Hopper): not measured; the one-column loss and the break-even count depend on the GPU's dp4a : int8-mma ratio, so it stays off until measured on e.g. a consumer sm_86 / sm_89 card. HIP and < sm_80: not compiled in.\n\n**Limits.** At 1 column the tensor-core kernel loses (fixed cost ~70 µs vs 60 on wq_b). At 8 columns it is still ~5x the weight-read floor (wq_b 100 µs vs ~17), so more rows a block or a pipelined loop could go further. Q5_K only; a Q8_0 twin would follow the same plan. Tested on random data at three shapes plus end-to-end token ids, not tensor by tensor on the live pack.\n\n**Checks** (as `.github/workflows/tests.yml`): `tools/check_hip_compat.py` OK; server tests 463 OK (12 skipped); installer and tools tests 584 OK (31 skipped) - the same counts as v1.1.35.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n","labels":[],"milestone":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"created_at":"2026-10-28T17:20:48Z","updated_at":"2026-10-30T04:47:06Z","closed_at":null,"merged_at":null,"merge_commit_sha":null,"assignee":null,"assignees":[],"requested_reviewers":[],"requested_teams":[],"head":{"label":"SeriousRising:ds4-q5k-imma-gemv","ref":"ds4-q5k-imma-gemv","repo":{"id":1437354424,"node_id":"R_kgDOVaxJuA","name":"project-maya","fu